WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.010
- [Host] This is Peach Fuzz.

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:04.860
Pantone's 2024 Color of the Year.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.330
It'll make headlines,

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.768
but we're not gonna talk
about it a lot in this video

00:00:08.768 --> 00:00:12.870
because selling colors is not
how the company makes money.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.630
It also doesn't make money selling paint

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:20.477
or color mixing machinery
or even color itself.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:25.530
What Pantone sells is
something much more abstract,

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.532
a promise of uniformity

00:00:27.532 --> 00:00:30.690
so that the color Peach
Fuzz looks the same,

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:35.010
whether it's printed out on
a billboard or a ceramic mug.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:36.180
This whole idea

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.020
that you can sell consistency
is relatively new.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.050
It's the brainchild of
this guy, Lawrence Herbert,

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.617
AKA, the king of color.

00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:48.843
This is the economics of Pantone.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.830
New Jersey, 1956.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.320
Recently graduated chemistry
major Lawrence Herbert

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.870
joined a small printing
company called Pantone

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.430
as a part-time printer.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.490
At the time, product
packaging was less consistent.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.290
Now we're used to seeing products

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.180
come in identical packaging,

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.820
like all these blue Modelo boxes.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.357
But Pantone's president says

00:01:10.357 --> 00:01:11.857
"It didn't use to be that way,

00:01:11.857 --> 00:01:14.700
"back when everything
was done over the phone."

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:15.667
- It's really difficult to say,

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:18.307
"Hey, that's a, it's kind
of like a cherry wrap,

00:01:18.307 --> 00:01:21.180
"but not as bright, but
maybe slightly darker."

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.760
It is really hard to
actually convey that message.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.700
- [Host] For example, see
these two camera boxes?

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:27.720
The yellow on the right

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.880
is brighter than the one on the left.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.020
Kodak says customers

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.750
were more likely to buy
the lighter yellow boxes,

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.660
logically thinking that
the darker boxes were older

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.820
and therefore contained older film.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.950
Customers didn't know that
the difference in color

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.260
was due to the fact that
the boxes were printed

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.000
at different factories.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.960
Each of which mixed their
own versions of Kodak yellow.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.630
Herbert recognized this was a problem

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.240
as he worked his way up
the ranks at Pantone,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.360
he became known for his
expertise in color chemistry,

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.670
and when he bought the company in 1962,

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.850
he had an idea to change
it from a printing company

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.250
into something entirely different

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.440
that could solve Kodak's problems.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.080
His team created a set of formulas,

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.800
so every factory printed the
packaging in the same color.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.270
- For a lot of companies,

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.790
color is part of their brand identity.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:19.830
There's Tiffany and they
have that specific blue,

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.637
and it's very important because we say,

00:02:21.637 --> 00:02:23.100
"Oh, that's Tiffany Blue."

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.520
We see the box and we go, "Oh,
you got me a Tiffany ring."

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.800
- [Host] 67 years after
Herbert joined the company,

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.960
Pantone has developed these formulations

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.270
into over 10,000 colors,

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.160
- So no one actually owns color.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.527
What we have is the IP of
the Pantone color system,

00:02:39.527 --> 00:02:44.250
which is a collection of
colors that we have selected

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.840
for it's not just this beauty,

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.110
but it's reproducibility
across different formats.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.880
- [Host] Matching colors across formats

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.500
is harder than it sounds,

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.480
but it's vital for companies.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.560
- They may have a color in mind
that they want to make sure

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.620
that can be reproduced
across different materials

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.850
that they may be presented color,

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:06.000
whether it could be from
their clothing line,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.820
all the way to the storefront,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.780
all the way to the internet
representation on the website.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.390
- [Host] So say a company
wanted the same color

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.890
on its packaging and its magazine ads,

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:20.790
the ink might look one way on cardboard

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.130
and another way on glossy paper.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:27.240
So Pantone would have to provide
slightly different formulas

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.940
to make the colors look exactly the same.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.580
For example, the cardboard
formula would be adjusted

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.240
to a matte, absorbent,
slightly textured surface,

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:37.920
while the formula for magazine paper

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.800
would be altered to account
for the shiny finish.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.290
To ensure all colors look the same,

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.800
Pantone has a rigorous
quality control process.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.770
The company uses a tool
called the Spectrophotometer

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.590
to compare the same color
on different surfaces.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:56.070
So Pantone would analyze
reflected light from the materials

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:59.340
and generate a number
value based on that color.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:00.990
Then they'd compare it to the number

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.450
for the color in their official index.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:07.140
If the data doesn't match,
they'd have to reformulate.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.610
- You know, basically someone's out there

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:11.790
checking these cards to
make sure that the fabric

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:15.090
that we've dyed are within a
certain Delta E difference,

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:18.570
meaning that it the color
is consistent one by one,

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.330
and it's highly, it has
to be highly accurate.

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:22.800
- [Host] Pantone has done color testing

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.660
for over 10,000 colors,

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.360
which it compiles into these Color Guides,

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.110
like this 1964 version,
which cover the whole gamut

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:34.230
or range of colors logged by the company.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:37.050
There are dozens of different
versions of the guidebooks.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.960
Some are even made specifically
for certain companies.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.750
- About half of our revenue comes from the

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.700
physical Guides that we have.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:47.070
Because there are so many
different formats of it.

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:49.110
- [Host] They can cost about $700

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.550
for this essentials kit

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:54.405
to about $9,000 for a set
of color display towers.

00:04:54.405 --> 00:04:57.510
Pantone says the guidebooks
generate the most revenue.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:01.500
It sells them to designers,
marketers, and artists annually.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.290
Each year they're updated with new colors.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:08.370
- Your book is exposed to
air and it can oxidize,

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.290
and then what you see in your book

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:12.840
can visually change when you look at it.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:17.400
So then you won't accurately
know if that's the exact color

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.050
that you are looking for.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.970
So for that reason, it is important

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.190
to keep getting fresh books

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:26.160
so that you can view them accurately.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.470
- [Host] Pantone says the
other half of its revenue

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.750
comes from its consulting and licensing

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:33.120
and digital services businesses.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.160
Universal Studios approached Pantone

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.350
for the 2015 movie "Minions",

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.540
asking them to make a new yellow.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.490
It made this banana inspired hue

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:43.350
and standardized it for the franchise.

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:44.430
- Banana?

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:46.864
- Uh, Stuart? (foreign language)

00:05:46.864 --> 00:05:47.697
- Banana!

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.910
- [Host] Companies can
trademark specific colors,

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:54.697
so you can't say, "Make and
sell your own competing products

00:05:54.697 --> 00:05:56.460
"using Tiffany Blue,"

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.140
but when it comes to all the other colors

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.460
in Pantone's guidebooks.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:02.970
- So those who get their
hands on the color,

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.800
of course they can use the color,

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.660
but when we're communicating colors

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.700
between different people,

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.820
and this is where the
Pantone IP comes into handy

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.460
because we can refer to a specific shade,

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.430
a hue of a color through its name.

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:19.260
- [Host] While the majority
of Pantone's revenue

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.000
comes from those branches,

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.890
it's well-known marketing campaign

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.650
might be what makes it a household name.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:30.600
2024's Peach Fuzz is inspired
by warmth and healing.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.240
A Pantone spokeswoman
said they anticipated

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.780
increased interest in
self-care in the next year,

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.240
which they felt the shade represented.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.120
- We want to make sure that
our community of creatives

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:42.953
and designers,

00:06:42.953 --> 00:06:45.210
you have a conversation
about color every year,

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:46.662
at least once a year.

00:06:46.662 --> 00:06:49.290
- [Host] After nearly
five decades of ownership,

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:53.730
Lawrence Herbert's family sold
Pantone to X-Rite in 2007.

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.860
The company makes color measurement tools,

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.790
which helped to cement Pantone
status as a color authority.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:01.020
Just five years later,

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.810
X-Rite was acquired by
the Danaher Corporation,

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:07.170
a life sciences and
technology conglomerate.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:10.410
In October, Pantone and
X-Rite spun off from Danaher

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:13.860
with 11 other companies
into a newly traded company,

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.570
Veralto Corp.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:16.560
While Lawrence Herbert

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.440
could have never predicted
Pantone's evolution.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.860
His idea was a leap forward
for the marketing industry

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.840
and the world of color.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.410
And as products advance
from physical to digital,

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:31.620
Pantone's scope will
have to keep evolving.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:32.670
- When it comes to color,

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.880
we want to make sure that it's
always going to be consistent

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:37.260
no matter where you are,

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:38.970
whether it's in digital format

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:41.280
where we can continue to expand on that,

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.020
those products and services,

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:45.090
or in different types of physical format

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:48.507
to help by our customers and
clients find the right color

00:07:48.507 --> 00:07:50.524
and be able to execute those colors

00:07:50.524 --> 00:07:52.403
the way that they want it to be.

00:07:52.403 --> 00:07:55.236
(whimsical music)

